WEBVTT
TITLE Wifi Csma Ca And Hidden Terminals — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
One radio channel, three stations: A, the access point, and C. Only one of them may transmit at a time.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A and C both start sending, and both frames arrive at the access point at the same moment.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The collision happens at the access point. Neither A nor C can observe it, because each is busy transmitting.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
A and C are both in range of the access point but out of range of each other. That geometry is called the hidden terminal.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Ethernet works because every station hears every other station, and hears itself at a comparable strength.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Collision detection: send and listen at once, stop the instant the two disagree, and jam the line so the others stop too.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
On radio the station's own signal at its own antenna is orders of magnitude stronger than anyone else's. The receiver is deaf while it transmits.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So 802.11 replaces detection with avoidance plus acknowledgement: send the whole frame, then wait for an ACK.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Physical carrier sense: listen to the air and decide whether energy on the channel looks like a frame preamble.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
A and C are 90 metres apart with a 60-metre range, so neither hears the other. Physical sense says the channel is free to both.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Virtual carrier sense: every frame header carries a duration, and every station keeps a countdown called the NAV.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
For a 1500-byte frame the reservation is 1141.5 microseconds: SIFS, the data, SIFS again, then the ACK.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Before transmitting, a station must see the medium idle for an inter-frame space. There are three, of different lengths.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
On 802.11b: SIFS is 10 microseconds, PIFS is 30, DIFS is 50. The shortest wins.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
DIFS is not a free parameter: DIFS equals SIFS plus two slot times, so 10 plus 40 gives 50.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Because SIFS is shortest, the ACK always starts before any waiting station may contend. The reply has priority.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A full exchange, in order: DIFS, a backoff countdown, the frame, a SIFS, and the ACK.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Add the parts for a 256-byte frame at 11 megabits per second: 50 plus 320 plus 192 plus 206.5 plus 10 plus 192 plus 10.2 microseconds.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
That is 980.7 microseconds for 2048 bits of payload, which is 2.088 megabits per second of goodput: 19 percent of an 11 megabit radio.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The published 802.11b table gives 2,088,246 bits per second for exactly this frame. This page computes the same number.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A station that finds the medium idle for a DIFS does not transmit at once. It draws a random whole number and counts that many slots.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The station with the smallest number transmits first. A collision happens only when two stations draw the same smallest number.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
For two stations the collision probability is exactly 1 over CW plus 1, which is 3.13 percent at CW equal to 31.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
With eight stations it is 12.04 percent at CW 31 and 23.18 percent at CW 15. The familiar formula is not the general one.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
After a failed attempt the window becomes twice the old one plus one: 31, then 63, 127, 255, 511, and 1023.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The mean wait is half the window, so it runs from 310 microseconds at the first rung to 10,230 at the last.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The ceiling matters: without it the window would double forever and a busy station could wait arbitrarily long.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
One limitation is structural: backoff cannot tell a collision from a frame lost to noise, because both look like a missing ACK.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Here is the hidden terminal drawn as a geometry: A to the access point 45 metres, C to the access point 45, A to C 90.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Both carrier-sense the channel, both find it idle, both transmit, and both frames are destroyed at the access point.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The repair: a short RTS, a short CTS from the receiver, and the CTS makes every station in its range fall silent.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The repair is not free: RTS 206.6 plus SIFS 10 plus CTS 202.2 plus SIFS 10 is 428.7 microseconds of overhead.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A different geometry: B is sending to A, and C wants to send to D. C can hear B, so C defers.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
C to D would not have collided with B to A: C is 90 metres from A, and D is out of range of the access point.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Nothing was destroyed. What was lost is capacity: two links that could have run together ran one after the other.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Hidden costs correctness, exposed costs throughput. RTS/CTS fixes the first and does nothing for the second.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: fix the variant, because slot, SIFS and DIFS all change with it.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: add the fixed overheads. DIFS, then the mean backoff of CW over 2 slots, then the preamble and header.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: add the frame and its reply, so the data at the rate, a SIFS, the preamble again, and the 14-byte ACK.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: divide payload bits by the total airtime. That is goodput: 5.5 percent at 64 bytes, 19 at 256, 57.9 at 1500, 67.8 at 2304.

